<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Deliberately NOT sandboxed, unlike the Team-signed build (which keeps
	  DropDrive.entitlements and its full sandbox).

	  Sign-in needs the keychain, and reaching it from an ad-hoc signed build
	  takes two things — this file is one half, LoginManager's file-based
	  keychain store is the other. Neither alone is enough; both were verified
	  on the real app:

	  1. A sandboxed app can't touch the keychain at all without a
	     keychain-access-groups entitlement, and macOS only honours that behind
	     a real Apple Team prefix. Self-assigning one (with or without
	     com.apple.application-identifier) makes launchd refuse to spawn the app
	     (RBSRequestError 5 / POSIX 163). Re-signing this exact build with the
	     sandbox back on reproduced it: the account chip went empty because the
	     stored session could no longer be read.
	  2. Unsandboxed but still ad-hoc, GoogleSignIn's own keychain store failed
	     anyway — it writes to the *data-protection* keychain, which needs an
	     application-identifier entitlement only Team signing provides. An
	     ad-hoc test binary gets errSecMissingEntitlement (-34018) there and
	     errSecSuccess from the file-based keychain, which is why LoginManager
	     drives AppAuth/GTMAppAuth directly and asks for the file-based store.

	  This build ships as a DMG rather than through the App Store, where the
	  sandbox is optional, so it comes off here. The remaining sandbox
	  permissions (network.client, files.user-selected) are meaningless outside
	  the sandbox and are omitted. The Share extension stays sandboxed — app
	  extensions must be, and it never touches the keychain.
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
